    "body": "### Problem\n\nThe documentation for `Q` to create a multicursor states \"Toggles a multicursor at the current cursor position\". When `virtualedit=all` is set, the cursor can be past the end of the line in \"empty space\". If a `Q` is used to place a multicursor while the cursor is in this position, a cursor is instead created at the end of the line (just after the final character).\n\nBefore making the multicursor (note `set listchars=eol:$` was used to make it clear where the end of the line is)\n\n<img width=\"828\" height=\"640\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/78563cd6-05b4-4484-8df9-d78c8460740d\" />\n\nAfter:\n\n<img width=\"828\" height=\"640\" alt=\"Image\" src=\"https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/d84d4d5b-7a5d-4548-bad6-2c1809b3ce75\" />\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n`nvim --clean -u minimal.lua`\n\npress `Q` to toggle the cursor and see it in the wrong place.\n\n```lua\nvim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { \"this is some content\" })\nvim.o.list = true\nvim.o.listchars = \"eol:$\"\nvim.o.virtualedit = \"all\"\nvim.cmd[[ norm 30l ]]\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nEither a multicursor created past the end of the line where the cursor actually is, or a documentation update to describe this limitation.\n\nMulticursors are implemented using extmarks which I believe are tied to actual positions of text in the buffer. Since `virtualedit=all` allows moving around the buffer and placing the cursor in non-text/content positions, this may not be possible to support due to the limitations of using the extmark API.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.13.0-dev-1528+gb7a0c71a54\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nLinux 7.2.3\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nalacritty 0.17.0\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nAUR",
